/*!
\page usage Usage & Testing

The design of `tapi` is to make testing simple and straightforward. Tests are often split into three
 categories, general testing, mocking and capturing, each having their own purpose and importance
 when it comes to testing code. This page describes these categories and how they can be used
 specifically with `tapi`, first starting with general testing.
\tableofcontents

\section testing_general General tests and Assertions

In this section we describe the importance of testing and how assertions can be used to make tests
 more robust. Testing is a fundamental part of any software project and is a fantastic way to ensure
 that the code you write is working as you should expect it to. There are many design and
 development patterns that make use of testing, but, `tapi` is often best used with test-after
 development where code is written first, and then tests are made.

Setting up a test environment/context is very easy, simply create your test file(s) and use
 whichever build system you prefer. Then, link your test executable with `tapi` by using the
 `-ltapi` flag for your favorite compiler, and finally compile your test file(s) with your source
 file(s) and you are ready to go.

To create a test, start with creating a context using `tapi_init`, then simply create a test with
 the `TAPI_MAKE_TEST` macro (_or the alternative `tapi_test_make`_), then add it to your test
 suite with the `TAPI_ADD_TEST` macro (_or the alternative `tapi_test_add`, see more below_),

```c
/* note: 'my_test' has to follow the tapi_test_func_t signature defined in tapi/tapi.h */
TAPI_MAKE_TEST(my_test) {
    /* arrange, act and assert. */
    ...
    return E_TAPI_TEST_SUCCESS;
}

int main() {
    /* add 'my_test' to your test suite. */
    tapi_context_t* context = tapi_init();
    TAPI_ADD_TEST(context, "my_test", my_test);

    /* run every test in your test suite. */
    TAPI_RUN_TESTS(context);
    return 0;
}
```

Assertions can be used effectively to check and compare results from your test(s), for example,

```c
/* from above... */
TAPI_MAKE_TEST(my_test) {
    /* arrange. */
    int x = 1;

    /* act. */
    int y = x + 1;

    /* assert. */
    TAPI_ASSERT(y == 2); /* this will fail if 'y' is not equal to 2. */
    return E_TAPI_TEST_SUCCESS;
}
```

Checks if the variable `y` is equal to 2 and will result in a failure if the condition is not met.
Similarly to how we use the macros, we can actually use the library functions to do the exact same
thing, mainly for preference. For example, we can replicate the above test _not_ using macros.

```c
/* same tapi_test_func_t signature as above. */
e_tapi_test_result_t my_test(void) {
    /* arrange. */
    int x = 1;

    /* act. */
    int y = x + 1;

    /* assert. */
    TAPI_ASSERT(y == 2); /* we can keep this for simplicity. */
    return E_TAPI_TEST_SUCCESS;
}

int main() {
    /* add 'my_test' to your test suite/context. */
    tapi_context_t* context = tapi_init();
    tapi_test_t* test = tapi_test_make("my_test", my_test);
    tapi_test_add(test);

    /* run every test in your test suite. */
    tapi_test_run();
    return 0;
}
```

\section testing_mocking Test isolation and Mocking

Assertions are great, but they are often not enough to test a functions logic and arithmetic. It
 is usually the case that a function that is being tested will call plenty of other functions, and
 if a tested function relies on function(s) that is not within the test, then in order to test our
 original function properly we must also assume that the called function(s) are working properly.
 This expectation of called function(s) to work properly can often be recursive, spanning across
 multiple functions, making test suites very complex, difficult to maintain, and even more
 unreliable.

One way to solve this problem is to mock the function(s) that are called by the function we are
 testing. This is done by creating a 'stub' function that will return a expected result instead of
 the actual function call, isolating the tested function from the called. In `tapi` this is done by
 using the `TAPI_MOCK_RETURN` macro to create our stub function, we can then add this mock
 and test to our suite using the `TAPI_ADD_TEST_AND_MOCK` macro (_which only works with a singular
 mock_).

```c
/* function being tested. */
float calculate_total(float amount) {
    float tax_rate = get_tax_rate(); /* we want to mock this. */
    return a * tax_rate;
}

/* create our stub function. */
TAPI_MOCK_RETURN(mock_get_tax_rate, 1.0f);

/* test using our mock. */
TAPI_MAKE_TEST(test_calculate_total) {
    /* arrange & act. */
    float total = calculate_total(100.0f);

    /* assert. */
    TAPI_ASSERT(total == 100.0f);
    return E_TAPI_TEST_SUCCESS;
}

int main() {
    /*
     * add our test and mock to our test suite.
     * note: the macro defined in tapi/tapi.h requires the format of:
     * TAPI_ADD_TEST_AND_MOCK(context, test_name, test_func, \
     *  func_being_tested, func_to_mock, mock_func);
     */
    TAPI_ADD_TEST_AND_MOCK(context, "test_calculate_total", test_calculate_total, \
        calculate_total, get_tax_rate, mock_get_tax_rate);
    TAPI_RUN_TESTS(context);
    return 0;
}
```

What if we want to test a function that calls multiple of the same function, or multiple functions
 that we also need to mock? In this case, we can use the `tapi_test_add_mock` function to add
 multiple mocks to one singular test. For example, if we have already created a test using
 `tapi_test_make` to test our previous `calculate_total` function, we can then add multiple mocks to
 it using the `tapi_test_add_mock` function.

```c
/* function being tested. */
float calculate_total(float amount) {
    int base_salary = get_base_salary(); /* we want to mock this. */
    float tax_rate = get_tax_rate(); /* we want to mock this too! */
    return (float) base_salary + a * tax_rate;
}

/* create our stub function(s). */
TAPI_MOCK_RETURN(mock_get_tax_rate, 1.0f);
TAPI_MOCK_RETURN(mock_get_base_salary, 50000);

/* test using our mock. */
TAPI_MAKE_TEST(test_calculate_total) {
    /* arrange & act. */
    float total = calculate_total(100.0f);

    /* assert. */
    TAPI_ASSERT(total == 50100.0f);
    return E_TAPI_TEST_SUCCESS;
}

int main() {
    /* create our test and add our mocks to it. */
    tapi_context_t* context = tapi_init();
    tapi_test_t *test = tapi_test_make("test_calculate_total");
    tapi_test_add_mock(test, calculate_total, get_tax_rate, mock_get_tax_rate);
    tapi_test_add_mock(test, calculate_total, get_base_salary, mock_get_base_salary);
    tapi_test_add(context, test);

    /* run our test with multiple mocks. */
    tapi_test_run(context);
    tapi_test_cleanup(context);
    /* ^ this is important! it is already included in the macro TAPI_RUN_TESTS, but we need to call it manually. */
    return 0;
}
```

\section testing_capturing Test output capturing

Another very popular way to test code is to capture the output of a tested function through various
 streams such as stdout and stderr. Although stdout and stderr are popular ways to capture output,
 there are many other streams that can be captured as well, such as memory and file streams. In
 `tapi` this is done by using the `TAPI_CAPTURE`, `TAPI_END_CAPTURE`, and `TAPI_DESTROY_CAPTURE`
 macros.

```c
/* function being tested. */
void calculate_total(float amount) {
    int base_salary = 10000;
    float tax_rate = 1.13f;
    printf("total is %.2f\n", (float) base_salary + amount * tax_rate);
}

TAPI_MAKE_TEST(test_calculate_total) {
    /* arrange & act. */
    TAPI_CAPTURE(stdout, 64); /* capture a max of 64 bytes from stdout. */
    calculate_total(100.0f);
    TAPI_END_CAPTURE();

    /* assert. */
    TAPI_ASSERT(strcmp(sink->buffer.data, "total is 10113.00\n") == 0);
    TAPI_DESTROY_CAPTURE(); /* free the capture memory (very important!). */
    return E_TAPI_TEST_SUCCESS;
}

int main() {
    /* create our test suite/context. */
    tapi_context_t* context = tapi_init();
    TAPI_ADD_TEST(context, "test_calculate_total", test_calculate_total);
    TAPI_RUN_TESTS(context);
    return 0;
}
```

Similarly to our general test examples, there are associated functions that these macros use to
 capture output, displayed below.

```c
/* function being tested. */
void calculate_total(float amount) {
    int base_salary = 10000;
    float tax_rate = 1.13f;
    printf("total is %.2f\n", (float) base_salary + amount * tax_rate);
}

e_tapi_test_result_t test_calculate_total(void) {
    /* arrange. */
    tapi_sink_t* sink = tapi_sink_make();
    tapi_sink_setdbf(sink, 64); /* set our sink to capture to a destination buffer. */
    tapi_capture_t* capture = tapi_capture_make(sink, stdout);

    /* act. */
    calculate_total(100.0f);
    tapi_capture_end(capture);

    /* assert. */
    TAPI_ASSERT(strcmp(sink->buffer.data, "total is 10113.00\n") == 0);
    tapi_capture_destroy(capture);
    tapi_sink_destroy(sink); /* free the capture and sink memory (very important!). */
    return E_TAPI_TEST_SUCCESS;
}

int main() {
    /* create our test suite/context. */
    tapi_context_t* context = tapi_init();
    tapi_test_t* test = tapi_test_make("test_calculate_total", test_calculate_total);
    tapi_test_add(context, test);
    tapi_test_run(context);
    tapi_test_cleanup(context);
    /* ^ this is important! it is already included in the macro TAPI_RUN_TESTS... */
    return 0;
}
```
*/